.TH "mbedded\-cbor\-advanced\-internals" "7" "2026-11-08" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH Internals
This page states how the library reads, for a person who changes the
library.
An application does not depend on it.
.SS 1. How the documents are tested
Every example of a guide in \f[CR]tools/site/guides/\f[R] is a doctest
case.
The cases of \f[CR]<name>.md\f[R] are in \f[CR]test/doc_<name>.cpp\f[R],
and their names start with \f[CR]doc <name>:\f[R].
The cases of \f[CR]advanced/<name>.md\f[R] are in
\f[CR]test/doc_advanced_<name>.cpp\f[R], and their names start with
\f[CR]doc advanced <name>:\f[R].
The cases of this page are in \f[CR]test/doc_internals.cpp\f[R], and
their names start with \f[CR]doc internals:\f[R].
A test builds the input that a comment of the document shows as text
with \f[CR]preferred()\f[R] of \f[CR]test/reference.hpp\f[R].
The examples of the reference pages of a group come from
\f[CR]test/doc_reference_<group>.cpp\f[R] through the snippet markers
that the comments of the headers name, and the names of those cases
start with \f[CR]doc reference <group>:\f[R].
A claim about allocations has its test in
\f[CR]test/allocation.cpp\f[R].
\f[CR]test/cts.cpp\f[R] runs the JSONPath Compliance Test Suite.
.PP
The reference site, https://asmod4n.github.io/mbedded\-cbor/, is built
from the comments of the headers through Doxygen, and from the group
pages in \f[CR]tools/site/reference\f[R].
Each \f[CR]example\f[R] block of a page runs in the browser, in the
library compiled to WebAssembly.
The build of the site runs each example and compares its result with the
page.
.PP
Each push runs nine Linux builds, two macOS builds and a test that
decodes the output of the library with other decoders.
\f[CR]test/run.sh\f[R] names the Linux builds.
With \f[CR]CBOR_FUZZ\f[R], CMake builds one fuzz target for each public
entry: \f[CR]decode\f[R], \f[CR]diagnostic\f[R], \f[CR]encode\f[R],
\f[CR]encoder\f[R], \f[CR]item_size\f[R], \f[CR]lazy\f[R],
\f[CR]path\f[R], \f[CR]sequence_reader\f[R] and
\f[CR]structs_decode\f[R], and with reflection also
\f[CR]schema_decode\f[R] and \f[CR]schema_encode\f[R].
.SS 2. A lazy reads heads
\f[CR]lazy::take_ownership\f[R] and \f[CR]lazy::view\f[R] read one head
at most: a leading tag 55799, which they skip.
A step reads the heads that it needs when it is called: the heads from
the start of its map or array to the target, and the head of the target.
It reads no byte after the head of the target.
So a target that the input does not hold gives
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] at the step.
A map or an array that claims more items than the bytes left gives
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] before the step reads an element.
.SS 3. A skip keeps a count
A skip reads heads only and jumps over the content of a string.
It keeps one count of the items that are still to skip, and no stack.
It stops with \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] when an item claims more than
the bytes that are left.
So the nesting depth does not bound a skip, and \f[CR]item_size\f[R]
uses the same skip.
.SS 4. The limits are constants
Each limit is a \f[CR]static constexpr\f[R] member of
\f[CR]cbor::limits\f[R].
A check of a string length or an element count stands under
\f[CR]if constexpr\f[R] on its limit, so a build that sets no size limit
has no such check.
A skip uses \f[CR]heads::decoder<false>\f[R], which checks no size
limit, because the limits count only what a call reads.
.SS 5. Tags 28 and 29
A lazy keeps no marks of tag 28.
It reads tags 28 and 29 as plain tags, so a read changes nothing in it
and needs no lock.
\f[CR]cbor::decode\f[R] resolves them, with a table of the tags 28 of
one call: a tag 28 enters the address of the node of its content right
after that node is made and before its children are read, and a tag 29
is a \f[CR]shared_reference\f[R] node with that address.
The vectors of the tree are reserved with their exact count, so no
address changes.
\f[CR]cbor::structs\f[R] decode keeps a table of its own and reads a tag
29 into no plain member.
.SS 6. A compile\-time path
The compiler parses a compile\-time path once.
At run time it is a set of constant arrays.
.SS 7. The item tree
\f[CR]lazy::clone\f[R] builds one \f[CR]cbor::item\f[R] for each data
item in a \f[CR]std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource\f[R] that the result
owns.
The children of an array or a map stand by value in one block of that
resource, so a container holds a \f[CR]std::span<cbor::item const>\f[R].
A text or a byte string is copied into the resource.
The first block is sized from the length of the input, up to a bound.
A count is checked against the bytes that are left before an element is
read, and the sum of all open counts never exceeds the bytes that are
left.
.PP
In the tree of \f[CR]clone\f[R], a tag 28 and a tag 29 are tag items
over their content.
The resource holds one sorted table: each tag 24 item with the root of
its embedded item.
\f[CR]cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item\f[R] searches the table.
The result is a \f[CR]cbor::owning<cbor::item>\f[R].
Its \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R] has a deleter that owns the resource, and
the function finds the resource through \f[CR]std::get_deleter\f[R].
\f[CR]encode\f[R] of an item writes each head with the additional
information that the item keeps, a float at the width of its type, and
does not follow a tag 29.
A NaN keeps its sign and payload.
.SS 8. The width of a float
\f[CR]structs::encode\f[R] writes a float in the shortest width that
holds it exactly (RFC 8949, section 4.1).
A NaN keeps its bits.
A \f[CR]double\f[R] NaN takes the half width when the low 42 bits of its
significand are zero, the single width when the low 29 bits are zero,
and else the double width.
A \f[CR]float\f[R] NaN keeps its own bits: it takes the half width when
the low 13 bits of its significand are zero, and else the single width.
The decoder pads the significand of a shorter NaN with zeros on the
right, so a NaN reads back with the bits that were written.
\f[CR]cbor::schema\f[R] writes a float at the width of its type and
copies its bits.
.SS 9. The schema reads the descriptions
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>\f[R] takes the members, the keys and the tag of
each struct from \f[CR]cbor::describe_members<T>\f[R], which
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE\f[R] or \f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS\f[R]
writes.
It needs no reflection to lay out, write or read a message, so one class
builds on every compiler.
With reflection, \f[CR]schema\f[R] also compares each description with
the public members that reflection reads, with
\f[CR]std::meta::access_context::unprivileged()\f[R].
Without reflection, Boost.PFR counts the members of an aggregate where
it is available.
.PP
For a list of fixed\-size structs, a compile\-time probe decides how
\f[CR]encode\f[R] writes the elements.
The probe is a \f[CR]std::bit_cast\f[R] of a struct whose bytes are zero
except for the bytes of one member at its offset in the message.
Where every member reads back those bytes, on a little endian machine,
the struct in memory has the bytes of the element.
Then \f[CR]encode\f[R] writes a contiguous list with one copy.
Else it writes each element field by field.
.PP
\f[CR]path\f[R], \f[CR]at_path\f[R] and \f[CR]decode\f[R] read a message
from both ends: the table from the start and the root from the end.
A check can fail on a message whose start is valid and whose end does
not hold the tag of the root.
Then they skip the items of the message once.
If the bytes end before the skip ends, the error is
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R].
So a message that ends too early gives \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] and
not the error of the first check that failed.
The skip runs on the path of an error only.
